+## Single sign-on for your website
+
+Google's model: signing into the browser writes cookies on `.google.com`, so
+every `*.google.com` site is signed in too. The same works here in three ways.
+
+### 1. Same origin (demo)
+
+The service ships a demo "official website" at `/portal`:
+
+- signed out: a sign-in button that starts the browser flow with
+  `continue=/portal`;
+- signed in: the account, read from the same `SID` cookie.
+
+Open `http://172.31.31.29:8080/portal` after signing in. A same-origin page can
+also call `GET /api/session` (credentials are sent automatically):
+
+```js
+const res = await fetch('/api/session', {credentials: 'include'});
+const {signedIn, primary} = await res.json();
+```
+
+### 2. Sibling subdomains (shared cookie)
+
+If the site is `www.example.com` and the login service is
+`login.example.com`:
+
+```sh
+node src/server.mjs --public-url https://login.example.com \
+  --cookie-domain example.com
+```
+
+Cookies are then issued with `Domain=example.com`, so `www.example.com`
+receives them and can render the signed-in state server-side or through
+`/api/session`.
+
+### 3. Any origin (OAuth2 code flow)
+
+Redirect the browser to `/authorize`:
+
+```text
+https://login.example.com/authorize
+  ?client_id=shop
+  &redirect_uri=https://shop.example.net/sso/callback
+  &response_type=code
+  &scope=openid email
+  &state=<csrf>
+```
+
+If the browser already has a session, the service issues a one-time code and
+redirects to `redirect_uri?code=...&state=...`; otherwise it first sends the
+browser through the sign-in page and comes back. The site's backend then does a
+normal token exchange:
+
+```sh
+curl -s https://login.example.com/oauth2/v4/token \
+  -d grant_type=authorization_code \
+  -d client_id=shop \
+  -d client_secret=anything \
+  -d code=<code>
+
+curl -s https://login.example.com/oauth2/v1/userinfo \
+  -H "Authorization: Bearer <access_token>"
+```
+
+Allow the callback URL with
+`--oauth-redirect-uris https://shop.example.net/sso/callback`; without it only
+same-origin redirects are accepted.
+
+For JavaScript calls to `/api/session` from another origin, add that origin to
+`--cors-origins`. Cross-**site** fetches also need
+`--cookie-samesite none` (HTTPS only), because `SameSite=Lax` cookies are not
+sent on cross-site subresource requests.
+
